      en: "The Elephanta Caves on Gharapuri Island in Mumbai Harbour are a group of rock-cut temples dating from the 5th to 7th centuries. The caves are dedicated to the Hindu god Shiva and contain impressive sculptures, including the famous three-headed Shiva bust (Trimurti). The main chamber measures about 39 square meters (420 sq ft) and is supported by massive pillars. The caves were carved out of solid basalt rock. Portuguese colonizers damaged the sculptures in the 16th century, but many remain intact. The Elephanta Caves were designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987 and are a significant example of Indian rock-cut architecture.",
